<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tsu Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1608 CE – 1871 CE · capital Tsu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Todo family in Ise and Iga, founded by the master castle builder Todo Takatora and famous for switching sides in 1868.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todo-takatora">Todo Takatora</a>, who had served many lords before attaching himself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, was transferred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyo-province">Iyo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iga-province">Iga</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-province">Ise</a> in 1608. He rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsu-mie">Tsu</a> castle and Iga Ueno castle and laid out Tsu as a castle town on the road to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine">Ise shrines</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The domain, eventually some 323,000 koku, was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todo-takatora">Takatora</a>'s descendants throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. Takatora was trusted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> as a builder of castles, including work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsu-mie">Tsu</a> benefited from the pilgrim traffic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-province">Ise</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iga-province">Iga</a>, administered from Ueno, kept its own traditions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>At the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-toba-fushimi">Toba-Fushimi</a> in 1868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsu-mie">Tsu</a> troops, stationed to block the imperial forces, instead turned their guns on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s army, contributing to its defeat. After abolition in 1871 the domain became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mie-prefecture">Mie Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tsu-domain.jpg" alt="Tsu Domain" width="320" height="400"><figcaption>Image: Pentacube (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tsu_domain.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1608 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tsu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Todo Takatora, Todo Takayuki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ise Domain, Todo Domain</dd></dl></aside></div>
